  "energy_arc": "Section 1 (Mukhada, bars 1-2): Heavy, brooding opening (6/10) establishing the Darbari theme over a mid-tempo gallop.\nSection 2 (Takeover, bars 3-4): Intensity surges to 8/10 as rhythm guitar claims the gat head in low palm-muted power chugs.\nSection 3 (Mukhada, bars 5-6): Sitar returns with fierce authority (8.5/10), charging the hook with deep gamaks.\nSection 4 (Taan Long, bar 7): Climax peaks at 10/10 with an explosive chaugun run cutting across driving rhythm chords to taar Sa.\nSection 5 (Outro, bar 8): Heavy cadential resolution (9/10) consolidating weight into an abrupt, monolithic unison hit on sam.",
  "rhythm_section": {
    "reasoning": "The key of E in Darbari Kanada anchors around Sa (S). For the mukhada (section 0), we establish the heavy groove using the slow chord plan S (8 beats), R inverted fourths (4 beats), and P power (4 beats). Under the sitar's sustained komal ga (g{R}[3] from beat 4 to 7), the guitar allows the chord to ring open, then locks into palm-muted gallops during active melodic passages. Section 1 is a takeover, which by rule is arranged by code and omitted here. Section 2 brings back the mukhada with 'same_as': 0. In Section 3 (taan_long), the sitar blazes in chaugun; the rhythm section provides full driving rhythmic momentum on S (8 beats), d (4 beats), and P (4 beats) with driving 8th-note palm mutes. Section 4 (outro) consolidates on colossal unison hits on S, locking with the sitar's cadential tihai descents and ending on a monolithic, ringing home Sa.",
